1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,107
What is a NIfTI image?

2
00:00:02,107 --> 00:00:05,898
Interpret the dimensions and spatial metadata stored in .

3
00:00:05,898 --> 00:00:07,162
nii and .

4
00:00:07,162 --> 00:00:07,584
nii.

5
00:00:07,584 --> 00:00:08,426
gz files.

6
00:00:08,426 --> 00:00:11,797
The goal is clear: Differentiate voxels and volumes.

7
00:00:12,647 --> 00:00:17,798
A four-D image is a time-ordered stack of three-D volumes.

8
00:00:17,798 --> 00:00:20,888
A voxel is a three-dimensional sample.

9
00:00:20,888 --> 00:00:31,190
An anatomical NIfTI is usually one three-D volume; a bold NIfTI usually contains many volumes along a fourth, temporal dimension.

10
00:00:31,190 --> 00:00:35,825
Header metadata records dimensions, voxel sizes, orientation, and timing.

11
00:00:35,825 --> 00:00:40,976
If dim4 is 240, the bold series contains 240 volumes.

12
00:00:40,976 --> 00:00:48,702
Spatial resolution comes from pixdim1 through pixdim3; pixdim4 often records the T R in seconds.

13
00:00:48,702 --> 00:00:53,853
Always verify the units in the header and JSON sidecar.

14
00:00:54,703 --> 00:00:58,086
Now connect the idea to an observable check.

15
00:00:58,086 --> 00:01:00,622
First, navigate to a NIfTI file.

16
00:01:00,622 --> 00:01:04,005
Next, run F S L info on it.

17
00:01:04,005 --> 00:01:06,119
Then, record dim1–dim4 and pixdim1–pixdim4.

18
00:01:06,119 --> 00:01:10,348
After that, decide whether the file is anatomical or functional.

19
00:01:10,348 --> 00:01:13,730
The command shown on screen is an example.

20
00:01:13,730 --> 00:01:20,073
Adapt its path to your own working directory and verify the target before running it.

21
00:01:20,923 --> 00:01:23,276
Quality control comes before interpretation.

22
00:01:23,276 --> 00:01:32,692
This example is a 64 by 64 by 40 image sampled over 240 time points with a 2-second T R.

23
00:01:32,692 --> 00:01:39,754
A useful self-check is this: What does dim4 = 240 mean for a bold image?

24
00:01:39,754 --> 00:01:44,461
The file contains 240 three-dimensional volumes in its time series.

25
00:01:45,311 --> 00:01:52,775
To recap: First, voxels make up volumes; volumes make up a four-D time series.

26
00:01:52,775 --> 00:01:57,572
Next, inspect headers rather than inferring dimensions from filenames.

27
00:01:57,572 --> 00:02:04,502
Before continuing, make sure you can explain this objective: Differentiate voxels and volumes.
